/* search — the corpus's first SCRIPT.

   Every other program here prints and stops. This one reads its own command line
   and leaves with a status its caller can branch on, which is the difference
   between a program that outputs and a program a shell can ask a question of:

       cufet examples/systems/search.cufe fox examples/assets/sample.txt
       cufet build examples/systems/search.cufe && ./search fox notes.txt

   The exit contract is grep's, because grep's is the one every shell already knows:

       0   something matched
       1   nothing matched — not an error, an answer
       2   a file could not be read, or the command line made no sense

   That 1 is the whole reason statuses exist. `if search TODO notes.txt; then ...`
   works only because "nothing matched" comes back as a status rather than as a
   sentence, and a status is the one thing a caller receives that is not text.

   Run with NO arguments it searches the file that ships beside it, so that reading
   the example and running the example tell the same story. A half-given command
   line — a pattern with nothing to search — is still answered the way a real
   script answers one: usage, and 2.

   Exercises:
   - `the arguments` — a series of text, EMPTY rather than void when there are none
   - `Exit` and `Exit with N` — from inside a function, unwinding through every block
   - `read all lines from the file` — the fallible form, handled once per file
*/

Bind void to usage:
    State "usage: search <text> <file>...".
    State "".
    State "prints every line of every file containing <text>, as path:line: text".
    State "leaves with 0 if something matched, 1 if nothing did, 2 on a bad file".
Done.

/* One file. Gives back how many of its lines matched — and a file it cannot read
   is a FAILURE rather than a zero, because "nothing here" and "I never looked" are
   different answers and only one of them deserves a 2. */
Bind number or failure to search-file, given (the text needle, the text target):
    Define lines as read all lines from the file target or pass the failure off.
    Define hits as 0.
    Define index as 0.
    For each line in lines, repeat:
        Increment index by 1.
        If line contains needle:
            Increment hits by 1.
            State "{target}:{index converted to text}: {line}".
        Done.
    Done.
    Return hits.
Done.

Bind void to play:
    Define needle as "".
    Define targets as a series of text.

    /* A pattern and nothing to search it in. This is the shape a mistyped command
       line actually takes, and the one place usage belongs. */
    If the number of the arguments is 1:
        Cast usage.
        Exit with 2.
    Done.

    If the number of the arguments is 0:
        State "(no arguments — searching the file that ships with this example)".
        State "".
        The needle becomes "fox".
        Insert "examples/assets/sample.txt" into targets.
    Done.
    Otherwise:
        The needle becomes item 1 of the arguments.
        Define index as 0.
        For each arg in the arguments, repeat:
            Increment index by 1.
            If index is 1, skip.
            Insert arg into targets.
        Done.
    Done.

    Define total as 0.
    Define unreadable as false.
    For each target in targets, repeat:
        Try to:
            Increment total by cast search-file on (needle, target).
        Done.
        In case of failure:
            /* Reported per file, and the search carries on: one unreadable path
               out of forty should not cost you the other thirty-nine's answers. */
            State "search: {target}: {the message of the failure}".
            The unreadable becomes true.
        Done.
    Done.

    State "".
    State "{total converted to text} matching line(s) in {the number of targets converted to text} file(s)".

    If unreadable, exit with 2.
    If total is 0, exit with 1.
    Exit.
Done.

Cast play.
